main idea of proofs is to reduce them to some known identities in the literature. This is achieved by direct summation or the constant term method. We also obtain some new single-sum identities as consequences.\n\n##### 13.$3$D Farey graph, lambda lengths and $SL_2$-tilings\n\n2306.17118\n\nAuthors:Anna Felikson, Oleg Karpenkov, Khrystyna Serhiyenko, Pavel Tumarkin\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHook length and symplectic content in partitions | ArxivLens (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/hook-length-and-symplectic-content-in-partitions-9750-1706185)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Paper ID: 2205.07322 ... @article{ballantine2022hook, title = {Hook length and symplectic content in partitions}, author = {Ballantine, Cristina and Andrews, George E. and Amdeberhan, Tewodros}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2205.07322}, archivePrefix = {arXiv}, primaryClass = {math.CO}, }\n\nBack ArXiv math.CO Hook length and symplectic content in partitions\n\nArXiv math.CO\n\n# Hook length and symplectic content in partitions\n\nCristina Ballantine, George E. Andrews, Tewodros Amdeberhan\n\n2205.07322 Published May 17, 2022\n\nChat with paper Cite 中文 View PDF Share\n\n### Publication\n\nPublished: May 17, 2022\n\nCategories: math.CO\n\n### Metrics\n\nSource: ArXiv\n\n### AI Quick Summary\n\nThis paper proves special cases of conjectures regarding combinatorial interpretations of hook-length and symplectic/orthogonal content formulas in partitions, establishing an equivalence between partitions with non-zero symplectic contents and partitions into distinct even parts. It also explores new partition statistics and their connections to distinct partitions and inversions.\n\n## Quick Answers\n\n### What is \"Hook length and symplectic content in partitions\" about?\n\nThis paper proves special cases of conjectures regarding combinatorial interpretations of hook-length and symplectic/orthogonal content formulas in partitions, establishing an equivalence between partitions with non-zero symplectic contents and partitions into distinct even parts. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Ballantine_C in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ABallantine_C+in%3Amath)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; In this article we prove the first three cases of the conjecture and several related truncated identities. ... We provide combinatorial proofs for all of these theorems and also for the pentagonal number theorem for PED partitions proved analytically by Fink, Guy, and Krusemeyer. ... Our methods are both analytic and combinatorial, and our results and conjectures intersect the areas of representation theory, analytic number theory, partition theory, and $q$-series. ... @misc{2205.07322, author = {Tewodros Amdeberhan and George E.~Andrews and Cristina Ballantine}, title = {{H}ook length and symplectic content in partitions}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2205.07322}, note = {arXiv:2205.07322v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... In this paper we add to the literature on the combinatorial nature of the mock theta functions, a collection of curious $q$-hypergeometric series introduced by Ramanujan in his last letter to Hardy in 1920, which we now know to be important examples of mock modular forms.\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2205.07322, author = {Tewodros Amdeberhan and George E.~Andrews and Cristina Ballantine}, title = {{H}ook length and symplectic content in partitions}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2205.07322}, note = {arXiv:2205.07322v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nThe dimension of an irreducible representation of $GL(n,\\mathbb{C})$, $Sp(2n)$, or $SO(n)$ is given by the respective hook-length and content formulas for the corresponding partition. The first author, inspired by the Nekrasov-Okounkov formula, conjectured combinatorial interpretations of analogous expressions involving hook-lengths and symplectic/orthogonal contents. We prove special cases of these conjectures. In the process, we show that partitions of $n$ with all symplectic contents non-zero are equinumerous with partitions of $n$ into distinct even parts. We also present Beck-type companions to this identity. In this context, we give the parity of the number of partitions into distinct parts with odd (respectively, even) rank. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Chern_S in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AChern_S+in%3Amath)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; The Andrews--Uncu Conjecture ... {T}he {A}ndrews--{U}ncu {C}onjecture}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2203.15168}, note = {arXiv:2203.15168v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... In this paper, we present a combinatorial proof of an interesting identity in their work.\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2208.09882, author = {Shane Chern and Dazhao Tang}, title = {{G}eneral coefficient-vanishing results associated with theta series}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2208.09882}, howpublished = {Advances in Applied Mathematics, 2024}, doi = {10.1016/j.aam.2024.102742}, note = {arXiv:2208.09882v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nThere are a number of sporadic coefficient-vanishing results associated with theta series, which suggest certain underlying patterns. By expanding theta powers as linear combinations of products of theta functions, we present two strategies that will provide a unified treatment. Our approaches rely on studying the behavior of products of two theta series under the action of the huffing operator. For this purpose, some explicit criteria are given. We may use the presented methods to not only verify experimentally discovered coefficient-vanishing results, but also to produce a series of general phenomena.\n\n  * Asymmetric Rogers--Ramanujan type identities. I. The Andrews--Uncu Conjecture\n\nShane Chern\n\nMar 30 2022 math.NT arXiv:2203.15168v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2203.15168, author = {Shane Chern}, title = {{A}symmetric {R}ogers--{R}amanujan type identities. {I}. {T}he {A}ndrews--{U}ncu {C}onjecture}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2203.15168}, note = {arXiv:2203.15168v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIn this work, we start an investigation of asymmetric Rogers--Ramanujan type identities. The first object is the following unexpected relation $$\\sum_n\\ge 0 \\frac(-1)^n q^3\\binomn2+4n(q;q^3)_n(q^9;q^9)_n = \\frac(q^4;q^6)_∞(q^12;q^18)_∞(q^5;q^6)_∞(q^9;q^18)_∞$$ and its $a$-generalization. We then use this identity as a key ingredient to confirm a recent conjecture of G. E. Andrews and A. K. Uncu.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProwse–Woodall (a:b)-choosability equivalence conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/375699/prowse-woodall-a-b-choosability-equivalence-conjecture)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # Prowse–Woodall $(a:b)$(a:b)-choosability equivalence conjecture ... Nandana K Vasudevan, K Somasundaram and N Narayanan, “List-Coloring and Chromatic-Choosability – A Dynamic Survey”, arXiv:2606.31702 (2026).\n\n# Prowse–Woodall $(a:b)$(a:b)-choosability equivalence conjecture\n\nLess than 1 year old · traced to 2026\n\nLet $L$L be an $a$a-list assignment on a graph $G$G. The graph is $(a:b)$(a:b)-choosable when every such assignment admits a choice of a $b$b-element sublist at each vertex whose selected lists are disjoint across edges. Let $(G)_b$(G)b​ denote the graph operation used in the source. Prowse–Woodall's $(a:b)$(a:b)-choosability equivalence conjecture. For all $a,b\\in\\mathbb{N}$a,b∈N, a graph $G$G is $(a:b)$(a:b)-choosable if and only if $(G)_b$(G)b​ is $a$a-choosable. The supplied text says this conjecture was disproved by Dvořák et al. using a 4-choosable graph that is not $(8:2)$(8:2)-choosable.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Na