ordlim: 200] Published: 3.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; 2306.17079 ... 2306.17081\nThis result, interesting on its own, provides the first relevant class of set systems for which the previous two notions are equivalent.\n\n##### 10.Embeddings and hyperplanes of the Lie incidence geometry $A_{n,\\{1,n\\}}(\\mathbb{F})\n\n2306.17079\n\nAuthors:Antonio Pasini\n\n## Abstract & Audio\n\nAbstract: In this paper we consider a family of projective embeddings of the geometry $\\Gamma = A_{n,\\{1,n\\}}(F)$ of point-hyperplanes flags of the projective geometry $\\Sigma = PG(n,F)$. The natural embedding $\\varepsilon_{mathrm{nat}}$ is one of them. It maps every point-hyperplane flag $(p,H)$ of $\\Sigma$ onto the vector-line $\\langle x\\otimes\\xi\\rangle$, where $x$ is a representative vector of $p$ and $\\xi$ is a linear functional describing $H$. The other embeddings have been discovered by Thas and Van Maldeghem (2000) for the case $n = 2$ and later generalized to any $n$ by De Schepper, Schillewaert and Van Maldeghem (2023). They are obtained as twistings of $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{nat}}$ by non-trivial automorphisms of $F$. Explicitly, for $\\sigma\\in Aut(F)\\setminus\\{\\mathrm{id}_F\\}$, the twisting $\\varepsilon_\\sigma$ of $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{nat}}$ by $\\sigma$ maps $(p,H)$ onto $\\langle x\\sigma\\otimes \\xi\\rangle$. We shall prove that, when $|Aut(F)| > 1$ a geometric hyperplane $\\cal H$ of $\\Gamma$ arises from $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{nat}}$ and one of its twistings or from two distinct twistings of $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{nat}}$ if and only if ${\\cal H} = \\{(p,H)\\in \\Gamma \\mid p\\in A \\mbox{ or } a \\in H\\}$ for a possibly non-incident point-hyperplane pair $(a,A)$ of $\\Sigma$. We call these hyperplanes quasi-singular hyperplanes. With the help of this result we shall prove that if $|Aut(F)| > 1$ then $\\Gamma$ admits no absolutely universal embedding.\n\n##### 11.Saturating linear sets of minimal rank\n\n2306.17081\n\nAuthors:Daniele Bartoli, Martino Borello, Giuseppe Marino\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2026-07-01&page=42&range=56)\nciteturn2search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: yesterday; @misc{2606.31703, author = {Jungkon Kim and Cheolseung Jung and Jong-Min Choi and Juseong Lee}, title = {{P}hantom: {A} {U}nified {F}ace-{S}wap {D}eepfake {P}rotection {F}ramework with {L}atent and {S}patial {C}onstraints}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2606.31703}, note = {arXiv:2606.31703v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... @misc{2606.31702, author = {Nandana K Vasudevan and K Somasundaram and N Narayanan}, title = {{L}ist-{C}oloring and {C}hromatic-{C}hoosability -- {A} {D}ynamic {S}urvey}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2606.31702}, note = {arXiv:2606.31702v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... The previous survey on list-coloring by D R Woodall (2001), emphasized defective choosability, the list-coloring conjectures, and different methods used for list-coloring.\n\n  * List-Coloring and Chromatic-Choosability -- A Dynamic Survey\n\nNandana K Vasudevan, K Somasundaram, N Narayanan\n\nJul 01 2026 math.CO arXiv:2606.31702v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2606.31702, author = {Nandana K Vasudevan and K Somasundaram and N Narayanan}, title = {{L}ist-{C}oloring and {C}hromatic-{C}hoosability -- {A} {D}ynamic {S}urvey}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2606.31702}, note = {arXiv:2606.31702v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nList-coloring, introduced independently by Vizing and by Erdős, Rubin, and Taylor in the 1970s, generalizes ordinary vertex coloring by assigning to each vertex its own set of admissible colors. A graph is chromatic-choosable if its list chromatic number equals its chromatic number. The previous survey on list-coloring by D R Woodall (2001), emphasized defective choosability, the list-coloring conjectures, and different methods used for list-coloring. This survey reviews major developments on list-coloring and chromatic-choosability, with emphasis on graph classes for which equality is known, graph classes exhibiting a nontrivial gap, and the principal methods used to prove such results. The survey covers embedded graphs, perfect graphs, complete bipartite and multipartite graphs, claw-free graphs, line graphs, powers of graphs, graph products, and selected variants of list-coloring.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2026-07-05&page=74&range=31)\nciteturn2search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; @misc{2606.31703, author = {Jungkon Kim and Cheolseung Jung and Jong-Min Choi and Juseong Lee}, title = {{P}hantom: {A} {U}nified {F}ace-{S}wap {D}eepfake {P}rotection {F}ramework with {L}atent and {S}patial {C}onstraints}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2606.31703}, note = {arXiv:2606.31703v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... @misc{2606.31702, author = {Nandana K Vasudevan and K Somasundaram and N Narayanan}, title = {{L}ist-{C}oloring and {C}hromatic-{C}hoosability -- {A} {D}ynamic {S}urvey}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2606.31702}, note = {arXiv:2606.31702v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... The previous survey on list-coloring by D R Woodall (2001), emphasized defective choosability, the list-coloring conjectures, and different methods used for list-coloring.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2026-07-08&page=169&range=56)\nciteturn2search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; @misc{2606.31702, author = {Nandana K Vasudevan and K Somasundaram and N Narayanan}, title = {{L}ist-{C}oloring and {C}hromatic-{C}hoosability -- {A} {D}ynamic {S}urvey}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2606.31702}, note = {arXiv:2606.31702v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... The previous survey on list-coloring by D R Woodall (2001), emphasized defective choosability, the list-coloring conjectures, and different me